---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About - Karim Bhalwani"
  description="Personal writing on AI systems reliability, agentic architectures, context engineering, and production systems design."
>
  <div class="container container-narrow stack stack-l" style="margin-top: var(--space-xl)">

    <header class="stack stack-xs">
      <p class="kicker">Profile & Philosophy</p>
      <h1>About</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        I'm Karim Bhalwani. I have a long background in building and evolving data and AI systems that are practical, reliable, and easy to operate.
      </p>
      <hr style="border: 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border); margin-block: var(--space-s)" />
    </header>

    <!-- Disclosure -->
    <aside class="disclosure-card">
      <p class="disclosure-label">Personal Blog Disclosure</p>
      <p>
        Everything published on this site represents my own independent thinking, learning, and analysis. The views, opinions, and any technical commentary are entirely my own. They do not represent, reflect, or relate to my employer or any organization I am affiliated with. This blog has no connection to my professional work or clients.
      </p>
    </aside>

    <div class="prose">

      <p>
        I enjoy learning new technologies and putting them to work in real systems. Curiosity is a big driver for me, but I try to balance it with discipline and validation. I like to understand how things <em>actually</em> behave in practice, not just how they are described. Trust, then verify.
      </p>

      <h2>What I Write About</h2>

      <p>This blog is where I think out loud. The recurring themes so far:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Agent control layers and harness design:</strong> What actually goes around a model to make it reliable, bounded, and auditable.</li>
        <li><strong>Verification and test integrity:</strong> Reward hacking, RLVR, the difference between a test passing and the software being correct.</li>
        <li><strong>Context engineering and token economics:</strong> Long context tradeoffs, hierarchical retrieval, routing intelligence to right-sized models.</li>
        <li><strong>Multi-agent systems and organizational change:</strong> Agent fleets, background workers, and what happens when AI changes team structure.</li>
        <li><strong>Data and privacy systems:</strong> Context-aware redaction, sensitive data flows, and building systems you can trust with protected information.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The focus is on clear thinking and practical outcomes rather than theory. I write for engineers and architects who are trying to make real decisions.
      </p>

      <h2>Purpose of This Site</h2>

      <p>
        This site is personal and independent. I use it to document concrete lessons from experiments, share architectural patterns that hold up in practice, and keep a record of how my thinking evolves over time.
      </p>

      <h2>Get in Touch</h2>

      <p>
        You can find my open-source experiments on <a href="https://github.com/karim-bhalwani" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a> and connect professionally on <a href="https://linkedin.com/in/karim-bhalwani" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LinkedIn</a>.
      </p>

    </div>

  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .disclosure-card {
    padding: var(--space-m);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-left: 4px solid var(--color-text-muted);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    background-color: var(--color-surface);
  }

  .disclosure-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xs);
  }

  .disclosure-card p:last-child {
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
  }
</style>
